1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,017
Water in an electrical room.

2
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:06,913
Weak generator batteries.

3
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:09,038
A broken announcement system.

4
00:00:09,088 --> 00:00:10,819
A faulty heat detector.

5
00:00:10,869 --> 00:00:12,657
Four separate incidents.

6
00:00:12,707 --> 00:00:15,184
But they all lead to the same place:

7
00:00:15,234 --> 00:00:16,907
the people evacuating.

8
00:00:16,957 --> 00:00:19,033
This is the butterfly effect.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,771
This is FireRisk Intelligence, built during this hackathon on Meta Muse Spark and SAM 3.1.

10
00:00:28,821 --> 00:00:32,390
It connects what a building already records:

11
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:35,662
logs, meters, assets and survey videos.

12
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:44,181
First, a rule engine links the records through known relationships.

13
00:00:44,231 --> 00:00:49,039
Grid power and the backup generator meet at a single transfer switch.

14
00:00:49,089 --> 00:00:54,401
From there, power reaches all six floors, and every floor's emergency lights.

15
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:04,178
Then Meta Muse Spark investigates, and tells the story.

16
00:01:04,228 --> 00:01:08,379
On October eighth, water was recorded in the switchboard rooms.

17
00:01:08,429 --> 00:01:10,213
It is still unresolved.

18
00:01:10,263 --> 00:01:12,699
A short could cut the grid supply.

19
00:01:12,749 --> 00:01:14,947
The generator must then start,

20
00:01:14,997 --> 00:01:19,859
but its starter batteries were reported degraded on September twenty-ninth.

21
00:01:19,909 --> 00:01:21,042
If it fails,

22
00:01:21,092 --> 00:01:26,782
one hundred thirty-five emergency lights and exit signs fall back on their own batteries.

23
00:01:26,832 --> 00:01:29,149
Newer ones last about two hours.

24
00:01:29,199 --> 00:01:32,226
For older ones, the real runtime is unknown:

25
00:01:32,276 --> 00:01:35,304
the monthly test lasts only fifteen minutes.

26
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,845
Every arrow states why two parts are connected, and on what basis.

27
00:01:43,895 --> 00:01:46,578
Every finding opens its source record.

28
00:01:46,628 --> 00:01:49,548
What cannot be verified is marked unknown.

29
00:01:49,598 --> 00:01:50,914
No speculation.

30
00:01:50,964 --> 00:01:52,221
Only evidence.

31
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,549
Muse Spark does not just summarize.

32
00:02:04,599 --> 00:02:09,810
It chooses its own lookups, like the emergency lighting supply of the basement.

33
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:13,680
Every citation is checked, and invalid ones are removed.

34
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:16,884
The official risk level stays with the rules.

35
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,748
Muse Spark also reviewed fourteen survey videos.

36
00:02:23,798 --> 00:02:30,192
SAM 3.1 outlines each finding in the frame, and the app links it to that floor's alert.

37
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:33,448
Video is supporting evidence, not proof.

38
00:02:37,199 --> 00:02:39,744
The manager gets clear next steps:

39
00:02:39,794 --> 00:02:45,120
inspect the switchboard rooms for water, and load-test the generator batteries.

40
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:49,939
Decision support, not a replacement for certified fire safety systems.

41
00:02:49,989 --> 00:02:51,792
FireRisk Intelligence.

42
00:02:51,842 --> 00:02:55,376
Because building risks rarely happen in isolation.

